@startuml blackboard-lifecycle

' ============================================================
' VISUAL THEME
' ============================================================
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam ArrowColor #57606A
skinparam DefaultFontName "SansSerif"
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF

skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  BorderColor #57606A
  FontColor #24292F
}
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFF8C5
skinparam noteBorderColor #57606A

title Blackboard Manager - Blackboard lifecycle (server-owned)

note as N_Scope
  **Scope** -- State machine for one `Blackboard`. There is no Run,
  no Phase, no chaining, no correlation concept on the service side.
  **Each Blackboard is fully isolated** -- a self-standing resource
  with this lifecycle and no inter-Blackboard relation. Server-owned:
  clients cannot bypass the FSM. The seal transition is what makes
  the contribution stream byte-stable and replay-able by client-side
  reducers.
end note

[*] --> OPEN : POST /blackboards\n(BlackboardConfig)

state OPEN
state SEALING
state SEALED

note right of OPEN
  - Accepts panel + slot declarations until first contribution.
  - Accepts contributions until seal request.
  - GETs return live counts.
end note

OPEN --> OPEN : POST /blackboards/{id}/panels (until first contribution)
OPEN --> OPEN : POST /blackboards/{id}/contributions
OPEN --> SEALING : POST /blackboards/{id}/seal\n(bb:seal scope)
OPEN --> ABORTED : POST /blackboards/{id}/abort

note right of SEALING
  - No further writes accepted (409).
  - Stream is being frozen and indexed for byte-stable read order.
  - Seal event appended to audit ledger.
end note

SEALING --> SEALED : success\n(stream frozen, audit committed)
SEALING --> ABORTED : irrecoverable error\n(persistence failure, etc.)

note right of SEALED
  - Blackboard frozen forever.
  - Contributions stream is byte-stable;
    `GET /blackboards/{id}/contributions` is deterministic
    for any subset filter.
  - Client controller MAY now run reducers client-side over
    the sealed stream.
end note

SEALED --> [*]
ABORTED --> [*]

note bottom of SEALING
  **Why server-side seal?**

  - **Single arbiter.** Multiple client KSs may post concurrently;
    a centralized seal removes the need for distributed consensus
    on "are we done".
  - **Deterministic replay anchor.** The seal moment is the audit
    anchor: everything before it is in the ledger and the stream;
    everything after it reads from the immutable, byte-stable
    contribution stream.
  - **No service-side reduction.** SEALING does NOT execute any
    resolver or merger. Reduction lives in the client controller,
    where it is a logged/replayable process over the sealed
    inputs.
  - **Blackboard isolation.** Each Blackboard is a self-standing
    unit. The substrate exposes no inter-Blackboard relation:
    no correlation, no chaining, no shared lineage. Any client
    flow that spans multiple Blackboards is responsible for its
    own bookkeeping out-of-band.
end note

@enduml
